Kampong Speu palm sugar, which has received GI certification, sold 12 tonnes at the end of this year's harvest, down about 30 per cent from last year.
John Sokha, an adviser to the Kampong Speu Palm Sugar Development and Promotion Association, said that in general, the GI palm sugar production season runs from November to May, and this year's production seems to be declining slightly as a family Quantities are starting to shift from GI palm sugar production to conventional production because it is less complicated and takes less time.
The price is also more expensive than regular sugar and the recognition of its quality is still limited.
This year, powdered sugar sells for 6,000 riel per kilogram, up from 4,500 riel per kilogram last year.
The price of palm sugar has also risen to 4,000 riel per kilogram, up 500 riel from last year.
According to Sam Saroeun, president of the Kampong Speu Palm Sugar Development and Promotion Association, another reason for the drop in sales volume could also be due to late orders this year, as there are two companies. Three just ordered in April and May, which the association could not produce yet.
For families who are members of this association, palm sugar production is the second most important business after rice.
Currently, the Kampong Speu Palm Sugar Development Association has a total of 142 families producing GI palm sugar, down from 172 families last year.
GI Palm Sugar covers two districts in Kampong Speu province, Udong and Samrong Tong.
Su Savon, CEO of CEDAC, whose company sells GI palm sugar, said that in general, many consumers are still unaware of the quality features of GI-certified palm sugar. No.
Foreign tourists to Cambodia in 5 months increased by 20%
Foreign tourists arriving in Cambodia in the first five months of this year reached 1.8 million, up 20 per cent from the same period last year, mostly from Asia.
Ho Vandy, co-chair of the Tourism Working Group, said that the main attraction for tourists to Cambodia was contributed to the opening up of trade, investment and good diplomatic relations with other countries.
He said that most of our foreign tourists are from countries in the region, especially Asia, which accounts for about 60 percent.
Recent statistics show that Vietnamese tourists were leading the period with a total of 342,122, followed by Korean tourists at 215,254 and Chinese tourists at 131,060.
Inbound arrivals also increased significantly during the same period, with a total of about 49 per cent, while the rest came by land and water.
A report by a leading research institute, CDRI, shows that tourism directly employs 350,000 people and boosts their incomes.
Last year, the sector brought in about $ 2 billion in revenue for the Cambodian economy.
Industry insiders say there are signs that Cambodia's tourism sector is diversifying into beaches and ecotourism, with the development of islands opening up new opportunities in the sector.
Mr. Ho Vandy said:
If problems in the Western economy improve, more tourists will come from there.
Chinese restaurant presence in Cambodia on the rise
In response to the growing number of tourists, Cambodia's food service industry is growing to serve local and international customers, and officials believe there will be more and more modern restaurants in Cambodia.
Food industry officials and operators attributed the recent rise to a surge in international tourists and domestic consumption.
Taing Sochetkrisna, director of the Phnom Penh Department of Tourism, said the number of restaurants in Phnom Penh increased from 340 to 370 between 2011 and 2012, while Beer Garden increased from 63 to 74 over the same period.
However, he did not dare to release the numbers in the first half of this year due to the sharp increase, and officials will observe the data collection before the release for 2013.
Among the growth, he noted, Chinese restaurants seem to be gaining traction in attracting Chinese tourists to Cambodia.
But some local investors do not focus on tourists, they target local consumption.
He said the Chinese symbol seemed to be on the rise as more Chinese businessmen needed more Chinese staff to work in Cambodia.
He said there are currently 20 to 30 Chinese restaurants.
Most of the chefs are Chinese, with training in China and Hong Kong.
Half of the customers are tourists.
They just think that in the past the country was at war and politically unstable.
There is not enough publicity from Cambodia in China.
Mr. Bou Chansery, Deputy Director of the Department of Tourism Planning and Development at the Ministry of Tourism, said at the seminar on the strategic direction of Cambodia-China tourism market in 2013-2018 that at the end of 2012, about 9 million Chinese tourists visited. ASEAN countries and mostly travel to Thailand, Malaysia, Singapore and Vietnam, while Cambodia receives more than 330,000 Chinese tourists and Cambodia is still a potential market to attract Chinese tourists.
At the end of 2011, the Ministry of Tourism indicated that Cambodia had about 2,000 restaurants nationwide, but only 907 were serving tourists, including 333 in Phnom Penh, 143 in Siem Reap, 60 in Sihanoukville and 371. In the provinces of Cambodia, it is open to serve tourists, while only 13 percent is set for tourists.
Bilateral trade between Cambodia and South Korea was worth more than $ 1 billion last year.
This is according to a Cambodian government official.
Koy Kuong, a spokesman for the Cambodian Ministry of Foreign Affairs, spoke to reporters after the signing of a $ 200 million loan from South Korea on Wednesday for infrastructure projects between Foreign Minister Hor Namhong. And South Korean Ambassador Kim Hansoo said last year's trade turnover was more than $ 1 billion.
The total bilateral trade between the two countries was worth $ 538 million in 2011, according to official data from the Korea Trade Investment Promotion Agency (KOTRA).
He said that Cambodia imported more than $ 900 million from Korea, while exports from Cambodia to Korea were more than $ 70 million in 2012.
The president of the Korean Chamber of Commerce in Cambodia, KOCHAM, previously told the Post that the rapid increase in trade between the two countries in recent years has prompted Cambodia to diversify its exports to Korea.
Many Korean businessmen are now coming to Cambodia to seek more opportunities, not only in the garment industry, but also in agriculture and construction.
The EDCF $ 200 million loan will help Cambodia pave the way for rural road development and infrastructure.
South Korea, which re-established diplomatic ties with Cambodia in 1996, said its government had so far pledged $ 378 million in concessional loans to 13 EDCF projects and provided 105 grants. $ Million through 38 projects.
Vespa sales in Cambodia are on the rise
Nine months after the Italian-made Moto Vespa returned to the Cambodian market, sales figures show the brand's domestic popularity has surpassed expectations.
From January to June this year, sales of Scooter Vespa increased, said Tes Dary, inventory manager of Narita Group, a distributor of the Viagra motorcycle manufacturer Piaggio Group. 70% of the target for 2013, mostly Vespa motorcycles.
The company operates factories in several Asian countries, including China and Vietnam.
Mr. Dary said that in the showrooms on Monivong Boulevard, there are an average of 20 to 30 guests every day.
But the price tag on the Vespa is not low, and sales are higher than Honda and Yamaha.
Cambodia's tax revenue increased by more than 20 per cent in the first half compared to the same period last year.
This is according to the figures obtained from the General Department of Taxation.
But experts say the numbers do not reflect GDP growth.
Sector tax revenue data showed that revenue from the financial sector rose 21.2 percent, the telecommunications sector rose 17.7 percent, the beverage industry rose 18.2 percent, the education sector rose 124 percent, the export sector imported goods. Garment increased by 32.2%, garment sector increased by 41.7%, consumer goods sector increased by 37.8%, architecture and construction increased by 14.3%, transportation and tourism increased by 39%, hotels and guesthouses increased by 25.9%. .
Keat Chhon, Minister of Economy and Finance, said in a statement released on Tuesday that the increase in tax collection was in response to efforts by tax officials to improve tax collection procedures. He asked the General Department of Taxation to improve its work more effectively.
Corruption is a factor, but building capacity and capacity to increase revenue is important.
Opposition parties claim that the government loses about $ 500 million annually through weak tax collection and corruption.
Data from the General Department of Taxation showed that last year the government collected $ 740 million in revenue.
Organic rice exports rise
Organic rice exports have risen sharply in the first six months of this year compared to the whole of 2012.
Yang Saing Koma, president of CEDAC, said at the opening ceremony of the Organic Rice Producers' Congress yesterday that organic rice had been exported by as much as 10 containers in the first six months of this year compared to last year. In the whole of 2012, there were only 5 containers.
He said demand growth was strong in the US market.
Keam Makarady, director of CEDAC's Environment and Health Program, claimed that organic rice was sold for $ 1,200 for bronze rice and $ 1,100 for brown rice.
He said that one container contains 20 tons of rice.
CEDAC's study found that organic rice cultivation without chemical fertilizers was more productive than other types of rice cultivation.
According to the report, organic rice farmers yielded up to 2.72 tonnes per hectare, while intensive cultivation yielded 2.37 tonnes and traditional cultivation yielded only 1.60 tonnes.
Son Kunthor, president of the Rural Development Bank, a state-owned bank for agricultural loans, said he was unaware of Cambodia's organic rice production, but said CEDAC was the largest producer of organic rice in the country.
Cambodia's rice exports up 125% in first half
Cambodia exported a total of 175,959 tonnes of rice in the first half, up 125 per cent from the first half of last year.
