Cambodia, Thailand agree to establish economic zone
Cambodia and Thailand on Tuesday agreed to establish two special economic zones along the border to boost trade and investment and to improve the lives of people along the two countries' borders.
Cambodian Foreign Minister Hor Namhong said at a joint press conference after the first meeting of the Joint Committee on Border Development and Cambodia-Thailand Relations that the two countries agreed to establish an economic zone. One is in Banteay Meanchey province bordering Sa Kaeo Thai and the other in Koh Kong province bordering Thailand's eastern province of Trat.
The two sides agreed to push for the construction of a 1,800-megawatt coal-fired power plant in Koh Kong province.
The two sides also agreed to develop roads and railways and set up four international checkpoints in four provinces along the border, with cooperation in a number of key areas, including tourism, health and combating transnational crime.
The living standards of the people of both countries will improve.
