Foreign investors' confidence in the stability of Cambodia's business environment is improving day by day.
In addition, effective contributions to the development of tourism, agriculture and construction are on the rise.
Rice exports in 2012 were about 200,000 tonnes, and the figure is projected to increase further this year.
The cost of construction projects approved by the Ministry of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction in 2012 doubled to $ 2.1 billion, and the sector is facing threats due to labor shortages.
At the same time, both the government and the private sector are working together to diversify Cambodia's export products and find new markets for Cambodian exports.
As a result, Cambodia is now not only famous for its Angkor Wat temples, but also for its recognition of some of its products, such as garments, fragrant rice, and other agricultural specialties, including durian pepper. Cashew, rubber.
Cambodian businessmen are benefiting from the free trade policy of doing business with foreign partners, and many foreign companies are coming to set up their own businesses in Cambodia.
In fact, in recent years, many traders and customers in Cambodia have had the opportunity to visit foreign trade fairs and exhibitions, which has led economic observers to say that trade facilitation is being carried out effectively. Its contribution to the global integration of the Cambodian economy.
Despite some shortcomings that it will take some time to address, many observers are optimistic that the Cambodian economy has many opportunities for development in both the short and long term. .
In the years to come, when power supplies, transportation and infrastructure are improved as planned by the government, Cambodia's economic development is strongly believed to continue. Sustainability and economic growth.
5-month rice exports rise sharply
Rice exports in the first five months of 2013 rose sharply compared to the same period last year.
Data from the One-Stop-Shop Secretariat for the Council for the Development of Cambodia's rice exports show that rice exports in the first five months of this year increased by more than 127 percent compared to the same period last year. Last year, it increased from 64,581 tons to 146,854 tons.
Exporters attributed the increase to the government's rice export policy, which is pushing companies to increase their processing and exports.
Kim Savuth, president of the Federation of Cambodian Rice Exporters and chairman of Khmer Food Exporters, said the increase was a surprise for Cambodia's rice export sector.
According to the report, Khmer Food is still the leading exporter, but the company exports mostly mixed rice, while figures show that most of the exported rice is fragrant rice.
The rice includes jasmine rice, jasmine, fragrant sage and ginger.
Currently, the price of Cambodian fragrant rice is $ 950 per tonne, while mixed rice is only $ 480 per tonne.
Kim Savuth said Cambodia had the potential to produce the rice, while some neighboring countries could not grow it.
According to Kim Savuth, Cambodia has an area of 1.2 million hectares for growing fragrant rice.
Hean Vanhorn, deputy director general of the Ministry of Agriculture's General Department of Agriculture and head of the One-Stop-Shop Secretariat for rice export formalities, said the increase in rice exports in the meantime confirms the success of the implementation of the plan. The four activities.
The four action plans include increasing rice production, facilitating agricultural purchases, streamlining export procedures and expanding export markets.
These works are carried out by the relevant ministries, especially the private sector.
The rice market requires the participation of the private sector.
He added that Cambodian rice is still at risk if some traders try to imitate the quality by importing rice from Vietnam to process and package using the Cambodian name, and on the other hand, the port of counterfeit rice is also a cause of damage to the market. .
Kim Savuth said that according to a study, Cambodia's rice storage from harvest to milling was up to 18 percent, a loss of yield.
He added that retention is also an issue for Cambodia, which could cause Cambodia to lose its competitiveness.
