Understanding seedling cultivation techniques
Although the vast majority of Cambodians are farmers, the country's agricultural sector is still relatively undeveloped compared to neighboring countries, with farmers still farming in the traditional and ancestral way.
Educating farmers on cultivation techniques is essential and must be done to achieve high yields and meet market demand to compete with neighboring countries.
However, in recent years, there has been increasing attention from stakeholders to promote the sector through community building and technical training for farmers.
The rainy season has arrived and farmers across the country have been preparing to plant rice.
At the same time, CEDAC has introduced farmers to the technique of sowing seedlings for transplanting, which will bring higher yields.
A CEDAC promotional article published on Wednesday outlines seven techniques for good seedling growth.
These techniques include preparing seedlings, such as cabbage seedlings, to avoid waterlogging, which leads to elongation but not growing well. Stick together because in order for the seedlings to grow and expand equally well.
The next step is after sowing, the farmer has to cover the nursery with the twigs and remove the twigs. More because compost makes the soil well moistened and conducive to growth.
The same article says that some seedlings need to be removed in the cramped area after they have sprouted so that the growing seedlings can grow equally well.
In the absence of rain, farmers have to water the seedlings like watering cabbage seedlings.
Farmers should mix water with cow urine or water compost to promote further seedling growth.
Finally, when removing seedlings for transplanting, they should remove the seedlings that are good for transplanting. Farmers should transplant one tree at a time so that each seedling is free to grow well.
Yang Saing Koma, president of CEDAC, said that more implementation of the above seven points is better than any other.
He said that although the yield was low, it was easier to follow the practices he used to do without having to transplant a single plant and to maintain it.
Only two or three families follow the technique.
