Foreign tourists up 18%
The number of international tourists to Cambodia increased by nearly 18 per cent in the first quarter of this year compared to the same period last year.
Official data from the Ministry of Tourism released yesterday showed that Cambodia received more than 1,172,000 visitors, compared to more than 995,000.
The number of tourists arriving at both international airports was higher by land and water, with 52.1 percent to 611,60.
Tourism growth in Phnom Penh and other tourist destinations nationwide increased by 34.3 percent, while Siem Reap increased by more than 8 percent.
Minister of Tourism Thong Khon once said that Cambodia no longer has a low tourist season like before, because more tourists come to Cambodia every month.
Ang Kim Eang, president of the Cambodian Association of Travel Agents, said the number of visitors could drop again in the coming months because of the rainy season.
However, he claimed that the rainy season in Cambodia has a beautiful landscape, which is an attraction for tourists.
According to the data, Vietnamese tourists ranked first with 1.8 percent, Korea with 7.2 percent and China with 55.1 percent.
Cambodia returns rice to the region
Cambodia's rice market is expanding to Asian countries to reduce reliance on a single market to avoid market risks and maintain export competitiveness.
Kim Savuth, president of the Coalition of Cambodian Rice Exporters, said yesterday that although rice exports to European countries, Cambodia's most important market, continued to increase, market share compared to total exports. Decreased.
Meanwhile, exports to Asian countries such as China and Malaysia are on the rise.
In addition, we have been successful in exporting to new markets.
This is because the quality of our fragrant rice is starting to be more recognized.
In the first four months of this year, Cambodia exported a total of 118,000 tons of rice, of which rice exports to Malaysia, Thailand and China combined reached 45,000 tons, accounting for about 34 percent of total exports. The country's total rice.
This is according to data from the One-Stop-Shop Secretariat for rice export formalities.
The data show that the three Asian countries are among Cambodia's five largest rice importers, while the other two are France and Poland.
Cambodia's rice exports have continued to grow every year after the government set a policy of exporting at least 1 million tonnes in 2015.
In terms of market competition, Cambodia has benefited greatly from preferential tariffs on imports to the European market.
An initiative by the European Union to help the least developed countries, that is, everything except arms, has allowed Cambodian rice to be imported into EU member states duty-free.
At the same time, rice imported from Thailand and Vietnam is taxed at around 175 euros ($ 224) a tonne.
Relying on a single market has been cited by economists as a risk to the market if those countries cut orders one day.
To alleviate the challenges, the government has also sought markets in other countries.
Several trade agreements with rice importers such as China, Malaysia and Indonesia to open markets for rice from Cambodia in the past are contributing to a greater market for Cambodian rice.
David Van, vice president of the Cambodian Rice Producers and Exporters Association (ARPEC), said new market potentials such as Malaysia and China were where Cambodian fragrant rice was flowing in and gaining traction.
Kim Savuth said that the current potential of the Cambodian rice market is not only in the European Union, but also in Asian countries if we have fragrant rice to supply them.
Phnom Penh: China has provided $ 400,000 in assistance to Cambodia to implement technical training programs on cassava cultivation and harvesting to meet market standards to increase export productivity, especially the ability to export to the Chinese market. .
The funding came yesterday in a trilateral project on the development of cassava production in Cambodia, co-sponsored by China, Cambodia and the United Nations Development Program (UNDP).
Speaking at the signing ceremony yesterday, Lv Zhouxiang, a representative of the Chinese Ministry of Commerce, said that seeing that Cambodia needed technical assistance to improve the quality of cassava products, China and UNDP decided to continue further cooperation. In addition to providing financial and technical assistance to contribute to strengthening cassava production in Cambodia.
She said the project would contribute to Cambodia's efforts to build its competitiveness in the market and diversify its exports.
The cooperation project will be implemented from now until the end of March 2014.
This is the second phase of financial support and the provision of technical training.
Cassava is the second largest agricultural crop in Cambodia after rice, and it also plays an important role in improving people's living conditions.
Last year, cassava was grown on a total area of 337,441 hectares, yielding a total of about 8 million tonnes.
Revenue from cassava sales is between $ 200 million and $ 300 million a year, including formal and informal exports.
This is according to figures from the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries.
Of the total exports, Cambodia accounts for about 90 per cent to Thailand and Vietnam unprocessed.
Nong Ratana, an analyst at the UNDP Poverty Alleviation Project, said that at present Cambodian cassava chips could not be exported to China due to the inability to meet sanitary and phytosanitary standards. In accordance with the requirements of the Chinese market.
So we hope it will contribute to strengthening cassava exports from Cambodia directly to China even more.
Today, Cambodia is the seventh largest cassava producer in Asia and is projected to become the fifth largest producer after Thailand, Indonesia and India, said Setsuku Yamasaki, UNDP Country Director for Cambodia. And China in 2018.
Export data from the Ministry of Commerce in the first quarter of this year showed that Cambodia exported a total of 245,000 tons of fresh and dried cassava, down 47 percent from the same period last year, which was 46.5. Ten thousand tons.
