ASEAN continues to seek nuclear power
Although Thailand and other Southeast Asian countries have postponed their nuclear power programs, further development is underway to improve the security of their infrastructure.
Thailand, Malaysia, Indonesia and the Philippines have postponed plans to build nuclear power.
They are under the International Atomic Energy Agency's ASEAN ANSN nuclear safety net following Japan's March 2011 Fukushima nuclear crisis.
He says Thailand should prepare to develop nuclear power even if it decides to postpone its nuclear program, which is set for 2026.
So far, Vietnam is the only ASEAN country to continue its nuclear power plant, which is expected to generate 2,000 megawatts by 2020.
Indonesia continues to examine the feasibility of building a nuclear power plant, which will build its first plant in 2016.
A feasibility study on Malaysia's nuclear power plant will not be completed until 2014, and construction of the first plant is likely to begin after 2021.
