Kampot pepper yield lower than forecast
Kampot: Pepper, which has a reputation for quality in Kampot province, yielded about 18 percent lower than expected, the first drop since receiving a geographical indication from France in 2010.
Yields under this target have been blamed by officials at the Kampot Pepper Quality Improvement Association for climate change, which has caused extreme heat and dried up water sources in the area, leaving pepper irrigated. Lots of falls.
Nguon Lay, president of the Kampot Pepper Promotion Association, said that according to the book, the Kampot pepper harvest was conducted from January 1 to May 31 every year, and so far the collection Already harvested twice, this year's yield was 22 tonnes, five tonnes lower than expected and one tonne lower than last year's.
He explained that too much fall during pepper flowering and fruiting could affect pepper crops.
Yields of pepper are expected to reach 27 tonnes this year on 12.5 hectares, while the total area under pepper is 41 hectares.
He says the association hopes pepper production will reach 50 tonnes by 2015.
At the same time, the price of pepper has risen, with black pepper selling for $ 8 for contractors last year and $ 11 for non-contractors.
Red pepper sells for the same price of $ 15 per kilogram.
According to the Post, in the past, so far, pepper farmers' livelihoods have changed a lot, some used to have only 50 or 60 clusters of pepper, he increased the planting to 400 to 500 clumps, and some always Thousands of clumps, but large companies have also increased their planting significantly in recent years, as in 2013 the planting area could increase from 20 to 30 hectares.
Kampot pepper received GI certification in April 2010 from France, which has made pepper products more widely recognized in the international market.
We have no irrigation water in March, when the peppers are flowering and fruiting.
She said that a large number of products are sold in the domestic market through supermarkets, and some are exported to foreign markets, especially in the United States and Russia, where the US market is estimated to absorb about 1 ton. Per year.
She added that she would dig underground wells for irrigation next year, without relying on water from natural streams.
