The Cambodian government yesterday accepted a $ 90.47 million low-interest loan from the Japanese government to implement a project to upgrade 83.5 km of National Road 5.
The formal contract came after a preliminary approval of the loan in late April.
The signing ceremony was attended by Mr. Keat Chhon, Minister of Economy and Finance, and Mr. Arakawa Hiroto, Deputy Director General of the Japan International Cooperation Agency (JICA).
Speaking after the signing ceremony, Arakawa Hiroto said the road improvement project would bring many benefits to Cambodia's economic activity.
It will not only make it easier to travel from Phnom Penh to the Cambodian-Thai border, it will also make it easier to connect the transport network from Thailand to Cambodia and on to Vietnam.
Private companies will benefit more from this connection.
Will contribute to economic growth.
Construction will begin in June 2015 and is expected to be completed in May 2018.
The interest rate on the loan is 0.01% per annum and has a repayment period of 40 years, including a grace period of 10 years.
According to the master plan in the project to improve the 83.5 km section of National Road No. 5, this is only the first phase of the total project, while the second phase is 130 km long and will be built from Battambang to Pursat. And the last stage from Pursat to the Prek Kdam bridge.
But the last two projects are still under study.
Since 1992, Japan has provided a total of $ 2.25 billion in official development assistance to Cambodia, averaging $ 120 million a year.
PM Criticizes Opposition Request for Commercial Banks to Lower Interest Rates
Prime Minister Hun Sen on Wednesday criticized the opposition for proposing lower interest rates on commercial banks to farmers.
Opposition officials' remarks were criticized by the Prime Minister at the opening of a new rubber processing plant in Stung Treng province, saying they were inconsistent with market practices and economic realities. Cambodia today.
Sam Rainsy Party lawmaker Yim Sovann said commercial banks in Cambodia charge high interest rates from customers.
Compared to other countries, this interest rate is high.
You can look at Thailand, Vietnam, they can borrow the lowest interest rates.
Now you can see that at commercial banks, some tycoons and traders can borrow at less than 1% interest per month because they have collateral, but farmers also have collateral.
When we reduce bureaucracy, I think we can reduce interest rates.
The reason for the high interest rates is not because of market prices, it is because of corruption and bureaucracy.
Prime Minister Hun Sen has said that lowering interest rates is killing banks.
The prime minister said the government was now working with banks to hedge their risk to provide loans to rice mills.
In Channy, CEO of ACLEDA Bank, the largest commercial bank in Cambodia, said commercial banks could not offer lower interest rates on deposits.
We can not provide loans with lower interest rates than deposits.
No bank in the world can offer a lower interest rate than a deposit, even in the United States.
In terms of deposits, the bank received $ 6.02 billion from 1.9 million customers as of November 2012, up 24 percent from a year earlier.
Cambodia May Benefit Shortly from Bangladesh Garment Industry Crisis
Phnom Penh: The recent collapse of buildings and accidents in the garment industry in Bangladesh has raised safety issues and boosted optimism that emerging brands will switch orders. Of them towards Cambodia.
Industry experts in Cambodia say the Cambodian garment industry has not experienced any major impacts so far, while some believe the problem in Bangladesh is likely to be long-term. At a time when it is trying to improve the industry.
Most of the factories here have long been under contract with buyers.
He said it was too early to say whether Cambodia would be affected by Bangladesh's garment crisis.
Previously, retailers worried about Bangladesh's safety standards left the world's second-largest garment exporter.
The sector accounts for 80 percent of Bangladesh's annual exports.
But for some retailers, it is very difficult to leave Bangladesh.
He added that retailers would start looking for new locations for their mass production if the situation in Bangladesh's garment factories did not improve.
Van Sou Ieng believes that the Bangladesh crisis is unlikely to have a long-term impact on Cambodia.
Production costs in Bangladesh are low, which attracts many retailers.
Cosmetics spend nearly $ 10 million in three months
Cambodia spent a total of $ 9.9 million on cosmetics imports in the first three months of this year, up about 130 per cent from the same period last year.
Imports of cosmetics in the first quarter of last year totaled about $ 4.3 million, according to a Commerce Department import report.
According to beauty industry experts, this increase is reflecting the growing demand for beauty products and the growth in this field.
Improved living conditions of the population, combined with an increase in the number of middle-income households, have also increased the cost of the product.
The cost of new skin care technologies is also increasing year by year.
So as the number of affluent and middle-income families increased, so did the demand for make-up.
The beauty business began to flourish in Cambodia during the early 2000s through the launch of a vocational training school in Phnom Penh.
Sun Heang, director of Christina Beauty Salon, said she had noticed significant differences between when she first opened her salon in 2002 and now.
At first, many people worked hard to earn enough money to cover their daily expenses, and then beauty was a secondary issue.
But things changed as their living conditions improved.
In the context of globalization, the people of Cambodia, a developing country, have been embracing Western-Asian, civilized, and especially Korean-style civilian makeup, which is becoming more influential than ever. On people, especially young people.
Ms. Ly Kimseang, General Manager of the Cambodian Beauty School, the first school to open a professional beauty training in Cambodia, said that Cambodia has a large population of young people and they love beauty. Especially women.
According to a 2011 report by the National Institute of Statistics, about 74 percent of Cambodia's population is under the age of 34.
This age is considered to be the age of strong economic activity, both in terms of income and expenditure.
Ms. Sim Sovanratana said that more and more young people dare to spend money to get beautiful.
Choice of floating dock
Region: The push towards the use of large offshore oil refineries for refining crude oil and LNG is moving forward and urges governments, including Cambodia, to rethink their growth strategies. After all, they give economic hope to gas and oil.
It is a global phenomenon, but one thing in Southeast Asia, where crude oil prices have soared, has improved economic activity, but it has been difficult due to a lack of port-like development and political difficulties.
This is the case in Indonesia, Cambodia, the Philippines and East Timor.
Due to rising oil prices, the government has rushed to open the door for companies to invest in the oil sector, such as in the Middle East.
But infrastructure such as roads, ports, railways, refining, storage and trained labor are costly, while the construction of pipelines across the sea, sometimes thousands of meters deep, is banned. Especially when oil prices fall sharply.
Some governments, such as Australia and Indonesia, are recognizing this fact with experiments using offshore oil refineries, which have eliminated the need for pipelines and are well-deserved access to resources in remote areas.
These technologies are being considered by Cambodia, Malaysia, the Philippines and Brunei, but some countries such as East Timor are not interested.
The floating dock is larger than an aircraft carrier and has the ability to refine and freeze LNG below minus 162 degrees, a requirement for exports.
The company claims to have received some understanding from Phnom Penh, but the Cambodian government has failed to develop infrastructure to access oil in the sea.
For many companies, the potential financial revenue for FLNG is even more important.
Last week, Dili accused Australia of being involved in the investigation and not acting in good faith during the 2004 negotiations on a deal for Sunrise development.
It is a new twist in efforts to reach an agreement on the payment of royalties between Australia and East Timor.
But Cambodia and many other countries in the region that hope for oil and gas are looking at this model closely.
Rubber cultivation in Cambodia will increase more than Vietnam
Stung Treng: Prime Minister Hun Sen has said that Cambodia's rubber plantation area will increase over that of Vietnam in the coming years due to Cambodia's potential for expansion.
Mr. Hun Sen spoke at the inauguration of a rubber processing plant in Stung Treng province yesterday.
We dare say so because we have an exchange of views with the Vietnamese side, which has run out of land to grow rubber.
He said the concessions granted to the private sector for investment were 1.5 million hectares, and that nearly one million hectares could be used for rubber plantations.
In Stung Treng province, 233 families have planted rubber on 2,290 hectares of land and after handing over their land titles, people will be able to cultivate not only Stung Treng but also this situation. Nationwide.
Mak Kimhong, president of the Rubber Association of Cambodia and chairman of Sopheak Nikar Company in Katot village, Kampun commune, Sesan district, Stung Treng province, said the project to build a dry rubber processing plant and develop a rubber plantation would cost a total of $ 26 million, $ 7 million. Dollars for the construction of the factory and other funds to spend on cultivation.
He said the company had already planted nearly 4,000 hectares of rubber on 10,000 hectares of concessions, and that the factory had started processing semi-finished rubber at a capacity of 4 tonnes per hour. The population can be up to 10,000 to 20,000 hectares in this province.
He said the factory would also collect rubber from locals and producers in Ratanakkiri and Kratie provinces.
When we came to plant here, people started planting a lot.
The prime minister said rubber cultivation had grown significantly through private sector investment and family cultivation.
So far, he said, private companies have invested in 118,439 hectares of concessions, formerly 54,000 hectares of state-owned rubber plantations and 107,669 hectares of family plantations.
A report released on Monday showed that the two Vietnamese rubber companies operating in Cambodia were occupying more land concessions than the legal limit, and that both companies were illegally logging in Cambodia.
Turn destructive areas into tourist destinations
Koh Kong: The mangrove forests of Koh Kong province that were once used for charcoal have now become biodiversity hotspots and tourist attractions.
He decided to visit Koh Kong because he saw the advertisement on TV.
Yem Yan, chief of Peam Krasop commune in Royal Khemarak City, said he did not know what percentage of the mangrove forest was popular with locals, but noted that tourists from many provinces visited the area. .
He said that the Peam Krasop community earns about 140 million riel from ticket sales per year.
The ticket price for a local tourist is 3,000 riel and a foreign tourist is 5,000 riel, and this income is used to develop the community.
The income of this community is not included in the government budget, but in the future it will be partially deducted into the state budget.
He said that the mangrove forest was destroyed in the 1990s because many villagers used it as charcoal for burning, but since the strict protection in 2000, the mangrove forest has become a place Ecotourism in 2004.
By then, locals had stopped deforestation to make charcoal, and they began to develop services for tourism, such as trading or boating, while some fishermen continued their normal work.
Figures from the Ministry of Tourism show that Cambodia received about 3.58 million tourists last year, an increase of 24.4 per cent from 2011.
Although some improvements have been made to keep the mangroves from being destroyed, getting people to give up their old jobs to find new ones is a big challenge and a difficult task. .
