He said that Sofia had organized tours for customers to the provinces, said that over the past three years, the trend of domestic tourism has improved.
Although Jim Kan is a farmer, he dares to spend some money for family members to visit this distant province, where he can see strange things that his province does not have and it is a relaxation for his family. Also.
Cassava exports fall 47% in first three months
Cambodia exported a total of 245,000 tonnes of fresh and dried cassava in the first quarter of this year, down about 47 per cent from the same period last year.
Data from the Cambodian Ministry of Commerce, released late last month, showed cassava exports in the first quarter of 2012 totaled 46.5 million tonnes.
Thailand is one of the two main markets for Cambodian cassava, along with Vietnam and China.
Meanwhile, agricultural officials in provinces bordering Thailand and some large plantation owners said earlier this year that Thailand had tightened export procedures, with more detailed controls on the quality of drying and storage of potatoes. This noodle also contributed to a decrease in farmers' sales.
Mr. In Sovann Mony, Director of the Office of Agronomy and Agricultural Land Improvement at the Battambang Provincial Department of Agriculture said:
They buy only good quality and hygienic tea, so farmers who do not take care of the tubers during the harvest and after the harvest can not sell or reduce sales.
According to export data from the Ministry of Agriculture, the momentum of cassava exports was mostly at the beginning of the year.
As of the first quarter, cassava exports totaled $ 11.7 million, about 30 percent of last year's total value of $ 38.6 million.
Mr. In Mony said that in Battambang province, fresh cassava costs an average of 260 riel per kilogram, while dried cassava costs about 650 riel.
In general, the price of cassava varies from province to province or from district to district, depending on the actual needs of the area.
Therefore, price instability and regional differences become a major challenge for farmers when they want to expand their land.
Te Haing, owner of a large cassava plantation in Banteay Meanchey province, said farmers generally did not have enough information on market demand and prices, and that they could lose out when traders lowered prices.
Prices fluctuate daily depending on the actual needs of Thailand.
At the fourth Cambodia-Thailand trade cooperation meeting held in Phnom Penh on April 22, Thailand agreed to reduce administrative regulations on the import of corn and cassava. Cambodia to make the export process easier.
The Thai government has a subsidy policy to stabilize the price of cassava to the population, which has set aside a total of 44 billion baht to subsidize cassava cultivation for farmers for the 2012-2013 growing season.
But according to a report in the Bangkok Post earlier this week, the Thai government will not continue the project as its domestic cassava prices rise as demand picks up.
On the other hand, the government also wants to help traders affected by the project.
First quarter oil imports fall
Cambodia's fuel imports in the first quarter of this year fell 12 per cent from a three-month period in 2012.
According to official figures from the Ministry of Commerce, Cambodia imported 4,121,190 tonnes of oil in the first quarter, compared with 471,000 tonnes in the same period last year.
Representatives from the private sector and government officials said the decline was not a sign of a drop in production, but was due to last year's high oil inventories.
Between January and March this year, Cambodia spent $ 397 million on oil, down 15 percent from $ 469 million, the data show.
Nguon Meng Tech, director general of the Cambodian Chamber of Commerce, said the country's trade activity was going well, but argued that a drop in oil imports did not mean the economy was not growing.
We still produce more for export.
So I think the decline may be due to the fact that most gas stations imported oil for storage during the fall.
Meng Saktheara, director general of the Ministry of Industry, Mines and Energy, said earlier this month that Cambodia could now receive hydropower from coal-fired power plants, which would help reduce oil imports.
