National Assembly approves agricultural community law
The National Assembly of Cambodia began debating and approving the Law on Agricultural Communities yesterday to promote agricultural development.
The law, which has eight chapters and 112 articles, was approved by the National Assembly in two chapters on Tuesday morning.
Article 4 of this law states that the agricultural community is a private entity, an agricultural economic enterprise formed by a group of natural persons voluntarily, jointly creating capital, jointly owning and jointly managing in a democratic manner. To strengthen the capacity of agricultural production, agro-industry, agribusiness, trade or related services, agricultural production to promote the economic, social and cultural members.
The Community Agriculture Law also requires the establishment of an Agricultural Community Policy Council headed by the Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, as well as officials from relevant ministries or institutions, community representatives, the private sector, and civil society organizations.
The Agricultural Community Policy Council has the functions and duties of studying, researching and consulting to prepare policy documents, policy considerations, strategic plans and action plans related to the development of agricultural communities in accordance with economic and social conditions.
Agriculture is a key sector for Cambodia's economic growth, and the government has set a policy of exporting 1 million tonnes of rice by 2015.
Statement of the Draft Law on Agricultural Communities, submitted by Prime Minister Hun Sen to the National Assembly on March 7, 2013, states that from 2003 to 2013, the Ministry of Agriculture Forestry and Fisheries have a total of 375 members with a total of 35,630 members and a total capital of more than 9 billion riels.
The statement continued:
Currently, the prices of Cambodian farmers' agricultural products are at the discretion of brokers, and the market also depends heavily on neighboring countries.
Chhay Thuor, head of the Neang Teav farming community in Kampong Cham province's Tbong Khmum district's Chik commune, which was established in 2004, said his community could help farmers negotiate prices with traders.
Neang Teav farming community was initially established with 40 members, but has now grown to 73 members.
LICADHO Director for Human Rights Monitoring and Protection, Ham Sunrith, said the move could make it difficult for farmers to form associations.
He added that two articles of the law set out the establishment, functioning and management.
Article 19, on the other hand, requires the community to register with the Department of Agriculture of the capital or province in order to obtain the legality of the agricultural community.
Khem Chenda, director of the Ministry of Agriculture's administrative department, said the registration was free of charge and that officials needed them to register because they wanted to help their products get to market.
Cambodia-Vietnam trade up 10%
Bilateral trade between Cambodia and Vietnam in the first quarter increased by more than 10 per cent compared to the same period last year, according to data from the Vietnamese Embassy in Phnom Penh.
Officials say facilitating cross-border trade between the two countries is key to the growth.
But they say growth has been slow due to more competition.
Tran Tou, an official at the Vietnam Trade Office in Cambodia, said the two sides were focusing on facilitating cross-border trade activities, and that the effort would contribute to the growing trend of general trade between the two countries. Both.
Figures show that in the first quarter of 2013, Cambodia's total exports to Vietnam amounted to more than 221.15 million US dollars, up from more than 201.19 million US dollars, while Vietnam's imports to Cambodia increased from more than 717.49 million US dollars. $ To more than $ 791.85 million.
Cambodia's main products exported to Vietnam are seafood, corn, dried tobacco, rubber, rice, cashew nuts, while the main products imported from Vietnam are all kinds of steel, grains, garments, rubber, vegetables and fruits, pepper, metal products, machinery and accessories. Move and so on.
