Can elections affect tourism growth?
Phnom Penh: Will the growth of tourists to Cambodia be affected as the fifth national election in July is approaching?
Officials and the private sector are optimistic that nothing will affect the growth of visitors.
Minister of Tourism Thong Khon says Cambodia's tourism sector has grown significantly in recent years, especially in 2012.
He said that currently 75 per cent of tourists to Cambodia come from the Asia-Pacific region, and that they would return to Europe when it was not raining so it would not be affected by the election.
They also want to see the election in Cambodia.
Cambodia has no risk and no impact on tourism.
Statistics from the Ministry of Tourism often show that the number of tourists dropped before or during the election year.
The decline was due to a political stalemate lasting nearly a year.
The tourism trend seems to be affected by the political situation, as the number of visitors increased very slowly by only 5.5 percent in 2008, with 2,125,465 tourists compared to 2,015,128 a year earlier.
Ho Vandy, co-chair of the Private-Government Working Group on Tourism Policy, said that in general, the 1998 election saw a drop in the number of tourists due to the outbreak of civil war in 1997, but elections the following year. In 2003 and 2008, there was no impact on tourism.
He said that politically mature leaders, including the use of word of mouth and the promotion of tourism through various media, have been able to raise the awareness of the people and the private sector. Collaborate with foreign partners to inform about this issue.
Report: Business environment in Cambodia is seen as risky
A report analyzing investment risk in Cambodia shows that corruption in the judiciary, human rights abuses and labor laws are major risks to seeking investment in Cambodia.
Cambodia's quarterly risk report was released on April 22 by global risk and strategy consulting firm Maplecroft.
The report shows that compared to neighboring countries such as Vietnam and Thailand, Cambodia has the highest risk index, but Cambodia has the potential for lower labor costs when in neighboring countries such as Thailand and China.
According to the report:
But the report says increased international investment in Cambodia has led to rapid economic development.
According to the company's risk indicators, Cambodia has the highest risks, such as in the business environment, labor rights and protection from vulnerability to climate change, high civil and political risk, macroeconomic environment, poverty and poverty alleviation. Develop human resources and framework regulations.
Currently, the rapid influx of Japanese investors to Cambodia sometimes indicates that some factors in the index are unlikely to reflect the facts of the case.
Due to the risk of corruption in Cambodia, the country is rated worse than neighboring countries such as Laos, Vietnam and Thailand, the report said.
According to Brimble, currently the implementation of laws and policy reforms to improve economic governance has not been well implemented, leaving some indices below par.
Cambodia also lacks effective dispute resolution mechanisms due to the slow pace of settlement and corrupt courts are perceived as a major challenge for the business environment.
According to the report:
Forced evictions, which are part of ELCs, are also seen as a serious blow to investors' reputations.
The case of Tate Lyle, a major British sugar company, is facing a multi-million pound lawsuit from Cambodian farmers for allegedly seizing land illegally as part of a business venture. Each is cited as an example of defamation for investors in Cambodia.
Cambodia's labor rights risk is higher than in neighboring countries such as Thailand and Laos.
Currently, a large number of fainting incidents at garment factories supplying retailers such as HM and Puma are cited as a risk to the brand's reputation. The company due to poor implementation of labor standards.
