Lack of quality of education in Cambodia as regional economic ties tighten
Farmer Pet Rath lives in Sampov Loun district, Battambang province, and his house is only about a kilometer from the Thai-Cambodian border.
He often crosses the border into Thailand to learn Thai at a high school in Khlong Hat district in Thailand's Sa Kaeo province, not far from his home.
Every time he stepped foot in Thailand, the state was amazed at the differences in the development situation of the area near the Thai border compared to his hometown.
While things seem to be very different between the two countries, the state observes that the schools and education system in Thailand on the Cambodian border are much more modern than those in his hometown.
Many teachers, some by car, some by motorbike, go to school on the easy paved roads.
This inequality in access to quality education does not occur only between Sampov Loun and Khlong Hat districts or between Cambodia and Thailand, but human resource development gaps are common in ASEAN countries. .
Inequality in the development of the education sector often occurs between less-developed regional member states and more advanced economies.
According to a new report by the United Nations Economic and Social Commission for Asia and the Pacific (ESCAP) released last week, the gap in human resource development in ASEAN remains particularly challenging. Between the old ASEAN member countries and the least developed countries, including Cambodia, Laos, Myanmar and Vietnam CLMV.
The study argues that this is a major concern as the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) prepares to establish the ASEAN Economic Community in 2015.
The same report shows that:
The report shows that the gap in development in the human resources sector is also a major concern, which could have a negative impact on the process of efforts to establish the ASEAN Economic Community, social, economic and cultural development in countries. Area.
As Cambodia prepares to join the ASEAN Economic Community in 2015, economists say strengthening Cambodia's human resource productivity and employment competitiveness is a must, otherwise international graduates High schools and workers in industrial plants may lose benefits.
Vong Samang, an economist and general manager of Soma Consulting Group, said Cambodia could face stiff competition when the AEC arrives because of the quality of its human resources. Cambodia is weaker in skills such as banking, finance, management and other skills than regional competitors.
Therefore, the president can decide to hire employees from Thailand or Singapore, which is more competitive to work as a manager or chief executive of the company.
However, he reminded that high quality graduates will get higher salaries because they will have more job opportunities.
