Problems of hiring based on gender
The Phnom Penh-based Paris-based modeling company has announced an online recruitment drive for the post of secretary-general in the office of general manager, but candidates must be women and have good looks.
A local real estate company also needs a sales manager, but must be male and healthy.
For a long time, many companies here, both local and foreign, have chosen a specific gender for the launch of their work in line with other criteria such as education and work experience by Almost no one objected.
But the practice is unlikely to comply with little-known labor laws.
The Cambodian Labor Law was passed in 1997, stipulating that companies could not recruit new employees based on qualifications that were considered less important than gender.
But many companies continue to do so, unaware that they are making illegal choices.
The real estate company announced the job in March on a local job site recruiting men for the position of sales manager.
He said that the sales manager has to go out to meet with many important customers every day, sometimes even go to the provinces until late at night.
Only men usually want to do this job because many women do not want to travel or work in the sun.
He added that it was much easier for men to spend the night away from home after meetings.
He said that while companies recruited women for other positions in the sales manager's office, they could earn up to $ 600 thanks to their commission from the sale of real estate.
Because they intend to work long hours and also work on weekends.
"But not everyone agrees," said Kolo Chen, deputy country director.
He says this is why his company's online recruitment campaign in January was looking for a female chief financial officer.
He said that they are very careful with finances because in Cambodian culture, husbands leave money to their wives to manage.
A senior legal adviser, who asked not to be named, said the recruitment announcement was against the labor law, but could be a huge gap in the development of the law in Cambodia.
Ministry of Labor officials could not be reached for comment.
An online survey shows that many countries in the region have similar regulations against gender-based hiring, including Japan, Singapore, Taiwan and Korea.
But an NGO official says hiring based on gender is discriminatory because many companies are judging first based on their own feelings about the suitability of candidates for the job.
Ros Sopheap, executive director of Gender and Development in Cambodia, said companies should briefly describe their work.
For example, by stating that the job requires a long trip or staying out late at night, candidates can decide for themselves whether to apply for the job or not.
She said that although there are many female students in schools who need home teachers, job postings should not be emphasized, especially for female teachers.
"It does not mean that male teachers will abuse students, on the other hand, these students are the ones who choose their teachers," she said.
But gradually, young people will fight against this discrimination because they know their rights.
Kampot: Salt production this year has dropped by almost 50 per cent compared to last year as production has declined due to the weather.
