Coastal tourists drop on New Year's Day
The number of tourists visiting coastal provinces during the three days of the Khmer New Year has dropped sharply compared to last year as Siem Reap hosted its first Angkor Sankranti event.
Tourism officials claim that this year's event in Siem Reap has attracted a large number of cultural tourists.
Soy Sinol, director of the Kampot provincial tourism department, said that tourist arrivals to Kampot had dropped by about 30 percent during the three-day Khmer New Year, down from more than 90,000 in 2012. At about 70,000 this year.
He said that the decline was due to important factors such as the familiarity of many people in Kampot, especially the Thansur Bokor resort, and the Angkor Sankranti event in Siem Reap also attracted many travelers to Siem Reap by abandonment. Excursion to this province.
Mr. Soy Sinol said that last year, the newly built Thansur Bokor resort attracted the majority of people who wanted to know, and then an average of 4,000 cars a day went up to the top of the mountain. Bokor, but the number of cars has dropped to between 2,300 or 2,400 per day this year.
Cambodia's attractive coastline is also experiencing a decline during this period.
Provincial tourism officials say the number of visitors has dropped by more than 20 percent compared to last year.
Estimates show that the number of tourists visiting Sihanoukville during the three days was just over 50,000.
Seng Kha, director of the Sihanoukville Provincial Department of Tourism, said he agreed with the claim that the Angkor Sankranti event in Siem Reap had attracted a lot of local tourists this year.
In addition, the gloomy weather and continuous rain before the Khmer New Year is also the reason why tourists do not choose to travel to the coastal provinces.
He said that the recent serious traffic accidents on National Road 4 could also be the cause.
Chhey Chhorn, deputy director of the Siem Reap Provincial Department of Tourism, which hosted the Angkor Sankranti event, said the province, which is rich in ancient cultural treasures, received more than 150,000 tourists during the three days of the Khmer New Year.
