A lot of people parked their cars west of Angkor Wat and walked.
Freight traffic through Phnom Penh Autonomous Port increased by 34%
Phnom Penh: Freight traffic through the Phnom Penh Autonomous Port in the first quarter of 2013 amounted to 24,677 Euros, an increase of about 34 per cent compared to the same period last year.
This is according to data from the Phnom Penh Autonomous Port.
A senior official at the autonomous port said increased economic activity in the country and increased exports of agricultural products to Chinese markets were a key factor in driving the growth.
Ieng Veng Sun, deputy director general of the Phnom Penh Autonomous Port, told the Post yesterday:
The main export items passing through the port are textiles and agricultural products such as cassava and rice, while the main imports are raw materials to support the garment industry, construction materials and consumables. Other uses.
