Cambodia conducts first agricultural census to obtain accurate data
Cambodia's first agricultural census was conducted to provide reliable information on this important sector of Cambodia.
Sorn Sithon, undersecretary of state at the Ministry of Planning, said:
The census consists of two phases, the first of which lasts 45 days, from April 1 to May 31, and the 22-day period from August to September.
The government, funded by the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, will collect data on Cambodia's agricultural activities.
In the second round, 5 percent of the total households will be re-interviewed on farming activities before experts review the data at the end.
The institutions involved in the census are the National Institute of Statistics, the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, and the Ministry of Planning.
Sorn Sithon said the total budget for the census was estimated at about 5.5 million.
The census topics include the total area of agricultural land, location, size, use and ownership of farmland, cultivated crops, cultivation techniques, water use and household characteristics.
