Warming affects aquaculture
Climate change is having a negative impact on farmers' aquaculture, especially in the dry season.
According to agriculture officials.
Rising temperatures cause water to evaporate faster and reduce fish production.
Say Sorn, president of the Siem Reap Fish Breeders' Network, said that daytime temperatures were rising, making it difficult for fish to survive in ponds, which are usually kept in deep ponds. Between 1.5 meters to 2 meters.
It is drastically reducing the number of juveniles produced by community members.
This problem is common for aquaculture fish farmers.
Van Po, president of the Takeo Fish Breeders' Network, said the summer heat this year seemed to be more severe than in previous years, which made the water too hot for the fish to survive. Pond water dries quickly.
Hav Viseth, director of the Department of Aquaculture Development at the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, said the current increase in daytime temperatures was a major factor in the actual decline in aquaculture production by individual farmers.
But Viseth thinks it will not slow down the country's aquaculture production this year as the number of farmers involved in fish farming has helped boost the country's total production.
Instead, he expects aquaculture production in Cambodia this year to increase by about 10 per cent from 2012.
He added that the number of fish farmers this year will increase to more than 60,000 families, an increase of about 4,000 families.
This will continue to increase aquaculture production in the country as a whole, even as individual household production declines.
