The trend of women in business is gaining momentum
Phnom Penh: The trend of women in business is growing, which shows that women are increasingly involved in economic development.
The membership of the Cambodian Women Entrepreneurs Association has grown to 150 after the Association for the Advancement of Women in Business was launched just over a year ago, from 14 at the end of 2011.
Representatives of the Women's Business Association said the significant increase was reflecting the growing awareness of women in teamwork and reflecting the growing number of women in the economy.
Ms. Seng Tak Neary, President of the Cambodian Women Entrepreneurs Association, said at the first annual general meeting of the Cambodian Women Entrepreneurs Association yesterday that the association was very pleased to welcome more new members. This will make a significant contribution to improving the business environment and motivating women to make business decisions.
The Cambodian Women Entrepreneurs Association was established in October 2011 under the initiative to support women entrepreneurs in starting and expanding their businesses by providing opportunities for women representatives in the business sector to address challenges. Governments, communities, businesses and the media aim to improve the business environment and facilitate the growth of women in constructive business activities.
Srey Sok Chenda, vice president of the Cambodian Women Entrepreneurs Association, said in a presentation yesterday that most of the 150 members were based in Phnom Penh.
Representatives of women entrepreneurs doing business in the food and beverage industry accounted for the largest share at 32 percent of the total, followed by handicraft enterprises at 10 percent in the form of training and Education is at 7 percent, while other sectors are secondary.
